#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940 :topic/math-academy-id 936 :topic/title "Converting from Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/math-academy-id 3173 :topic/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db88b087-b136-58ed-9ecd-7896e0822fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r^{2}\\cos (2θ) + r^{2}\\sin (2θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122835} {:answer/id #uuid "f39a24b5-23ab-5f9d-bfe3-258ca717c94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r^{2}\\cos (2θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122836} {:answer/id #uuid "2a439bde-7060-5f47-907a-df53e7c751d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\sin (2θ) + 2r\\sin^{2} θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122837} {:answer/id #uuid "2edc2883-3d8d-53a0-bb5d-d106db10244f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\cos (2θ) + r\\sin (2θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122838} {:answer/id #uuid "74f961ba-683e-50da-8c1a-e63f2d0ae676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\cos θ + r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122839}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a86a93b6-feda-5a79-b8d8-74c4acc61388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79830e35-fb58-5c0c-8ffe-3455034ae32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368497} {:answer/id #uuid "e18a672b-de77-5df8-8f1b-84076247b048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368498} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7be39b-6734-5fbb-b31d-4ee6cfc206ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368499} {:answer/id #uuid "633b9946-97ef-5a0d-a217-10b115c3dbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368500} {:answer/id #uuid "1cddd4cb-d4e0-5346-8409-af4fe2ea10fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cddd4cb-d4e0-5346-8409-af4fe2ea10fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368501} :answer-field/id #uuid "77fe4fb6-8827-5540-a442-722c8b1aa5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0722c4ed-9b45-5a82-9aca-63ef2538a12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37192" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d87c61-ce4c-51b4-a48d-e8a1bd1e34a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122842} {:answer/id #uuid "e8df0f85-5951-5810-997c-6d9f9ec513ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122843} {:answer/id #uuid "29657135-0af4-5058-ad50-f0440efad395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r\\cos θ + r\\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122844} {:answer/id #uuid "2bad1c34-a8db-52f0-b044-93c8515a9174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122845}] :answer-field/id #uuid "086dfdbb-92b2-5cae-9238-3f1a3c6feab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840}] :question/id #uuid "08b52ee2-9e81-5a5f-aeda-8fc812032d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-37196" :question/problem "Given that $g(x, y) = \\ln (x + y)$, find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r}$, where $(x, y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7120b822-0ff6-547d-b7c2-2241a49aca6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358738} {:answer/id #uuid "7d848135-2b15-5316-9a46-e4e3b36ec08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358845} {:answer/id #uuid "811e3aba-e8b3-5975-bf14-431f1c3fe93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358879} {:answer/id #uuid "9029ca1e-4b6a-555a-8f91-25f0d427d82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359010} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b0a061-fad6-5c22-b008-8d6182556cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9029ca1e-4b6a-555a-8f91-25f0d427d82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359010} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d76e27a-bad6-5130-9b46-77d20a6d4839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360203}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4970bf30-0b07-5dcd-b3de-478f21daf6db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94999" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dce7e8f-7060-53e3-85cb-4987af63f4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358164} {:answer/id #uuid "6202b197-e3e5-5891-8722-848e41ddd428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358606} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc5a7fb-705f-5c74-b33e-9a9c4a94bf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358965} {:answer/id #uuid "e6251725-fa38-5c2d-a0b0-c05928f16370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359789} {:answer/id #uuid "e704bf2b-93ca-5b8f-8349-92ea61dd3fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6251725-fa38-5c2d-a0b0-c05928f16370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359789} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbcd748-fe7e-5cef-acf6-5edffcf9bb9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3f6701f-c993-5ff2-926d-7219617af2de" :question/math-academy-id "q-95109" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5cf6f4-8993-5a59-935b-fbd20502b0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358151} {:answer/id #uuid "51d099de-8e46-57d2-b3a9-1bf43d6107d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358456} {:answer/id #uuid "5c370ba9-4b6a-5155-8e84-b69d91cecfd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358553} {:answer/id #uuid "6b781cf2-8fa4-56d4-825d-5429ed24f588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358695} {:answer/id #uuid "abad9f8c-0b50-5c33-9de5-9335756322a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5cf6f4-8993-5a59-935b-fbd20502b0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358151} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5464eca-9902-5070-94ce-68bcd1dd8713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360403}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bc0127e-5521-50db-8fdb-586719893922" :question/math-academy-id "q-95112" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +0\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255b1439-4503-5ba1-9467-b4cbf23cf837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358086} {:answer/id #uuid "2826740b-006a-5fb8-af1b-b04cb4e430c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358112} {:answer/id #uuid "8f20dc0c-2f9c-59cb-9371-1e2bab94de58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358999} {:answer/id #uuid "c17a4d57-ab7f-5cad-9c18-15918d10a4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359449} {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14ca8c8-8191-546e-952e-035f83d5d2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a5ea0e8-c2a8-56bd-bc0a-1183f811a786" :question/math-academy-id "q-95113" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe46cca-410f-5c55-88ef-8d80e7031ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358726} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7b642c-5011-5fe6-b8b3-8ba15e13f44b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359374} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} {:answer/id #uuid "e32325f6-ab86-53be-a9ca-0e5883831318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359761} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9162b2-a7fc-5e49-88a2-4b87f9188d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebf0a96-067c-5319-889e-a652fa3fcdea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360139}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09b6c584-862f-5f2c-8d59-a960b719bfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95116" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id