/value "2\\sin θ - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124085} {:answer/id #uuid "4bfce416-a298-522a-87e6-f3f3f8bac492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124086} {:answer/id #uuid "4d92d4a2-d497-5657-9600-57ab1977ae32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin θ + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124087}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b6129c-b733-5c71-92b5-462c14e827e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124082}] :question/id #uuid "b5abfa64-ddd6-5ad1-8a8c-38f9b879752f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95001" :question/problem "Given that $v(r, θ) = r(2 + \\cos θ)$, find $\\frac{∂v}{∂x}$, where $(x, y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5931fbcb-d5d0-514f-9a40-b9dacd7f5ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282447} {:answer/id #uuid "5b96e589-82a0-56f3-8019-45d38ccf7778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282465} {:answer/id #uuid "9af211ae-d2c1-5419-914b-d99b2a2e61c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283040} {:answer/id #uuid "c7155d84-c3f7-59e4-acae-f39485ce9da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283422} {:answer/id #uuid "e377d96d-2e54-5472-ad65-76a95cf2bbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e377d96d-2e54-5472-ad65-76a95cf2bbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283673} :answer-field/id #uuid "2670f341-82e8-5d70-983c-019cc825c363" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b54313ba-f7d3-5825-b960-2ab13d62a75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-165282" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd315d7-b1d5-551a-af37-e14e882123b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358153} {:answer/id #uuid "421e4508-c098-51e8-90e8-98bf10fa5694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358313} {:answer/id #uuid "7d38dabb-3b90-5940-a75d-8510ce424b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358842} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e4c0a7-a55b-5eab-8f76-5e79031a6d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359587} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dd87cb-765e-56e5-b2a7-b887923ea35e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1e4c0a7-a55b-5eab-8f76-5e79031a6d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359587} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d594cdf-96e3-59ea-b073-9e09cf2903a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360268}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "089e9487-84e2-5cf6-99a6-f3b1375b3cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-95129" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=2r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=2,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5558a820-fe68-5154-b605-abf1888ca002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358495} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccd388d-c735-55eb-b378-f7d1e760c7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358704} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7beea8-2165-59f1-b2bd-041b16b78155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358854} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb8186e-5270-5422-93d1-cdc25d726dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359269} {:answer/id #uuid "dbba7a33-6727-5aa3-8d25-973cb0049ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbba7a33-6727-5aa3-8d25-973cb0049ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359678} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0ec5fc-3755-5a5d-a8d1-a936b1188676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360350}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4ca4975-1827-563d-9fb6-1ff2551b2f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-95130" :question/problem "Given that $g(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }=-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52cf3c7c-f856-5e1a-893d-423bede6de59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358463} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6451f3-ba0d-5b74-abb5-e944654eaa92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358982} {:answer/id #uuid "a99fe949-6ed3-526e-8127-1b1ec443989e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359214} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cb342c-cb70-5a1b-b38b-8f594ea8d85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359595} {:answer/id #uuid "d540cc2e-6105-5828-a5a3-41026567f180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2cb342c-cb70-5a1b-b38b-8f594ea8d85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359595} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eeefd3d-0951-5ce0-8878-fb3b5684db7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a4e990c-7d6e-5ded-b574-687e77d66cbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95132" :question/problem "Given that $f(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta }=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a1ee6a-caac-58ee-afb5-38bc8bb1ed51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357819} {:answer/id #uuid "2228fe89-b437-5a7a-aa61-67383a3bc1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358049} {:answer/id #uuid "2486a9ac-6c70-5c04-9668-5c8f9093b469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358077} {:answer/id #uuid "c28adbb2-a46a-5fcd-bc0c-7c33f8291c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359459} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4bc83b-09f3-5b22-b701-0b449bf034fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2228fe89-b437-5a7a-aa61-67383a3bc1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358049} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b690a60-fc65-5545-974d-063013346d86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0702360a-d22f-5e34-a49e-3610cfb8aefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-95133" :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "812a2a72-c787-5aec-b20b-53b5609d7060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368880} {:answer/id #uuid "57701b6b-149a-570c-8820-a5a4899a9926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368881} {:answer/id #uuid "7956c7d2-5ce8-5570-912d-71126718b86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368882} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6f8124-2427-51e4-b3d5-8a7f5cc0bfee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368883} {:answer/id #uuid "29dabe43-58c4-5883-9567-12a537018918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29dabe43-58c4-5883-9567-12a537018918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368884} :answer-field/id #uuid "63ac7f03-12c3-54a9-b69c-fc2e73fe5c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368879}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6f35556-89a1-5c4e-ad2e-ad3a5182772a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95020" :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }=4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60efe842-a152-5a8d-94b4-e0b50381d44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368887} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c358c5-25b8-5374-9d9d-f36d756d1533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368888} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ab60a0-c20b-514b-93f7-be5b89a2ed07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368889} {:answer/id #uuid "4f084c23-67fc-5831-a5d3-607d0f3f2651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368890} {:answer/id #uuid "c7fe4eeb-f778-583c-9aed-a3ec394140b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c358c5-25b8-5374-9d9d-f36d756d1533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368888} :answer-field/id #uuid "208a9196-43b6-5acc-b9b2-19dc5589219d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4815c77-06a6-5342-87f5-965d49f4d616" :question/math-academy-id "q-95134" :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta )=r(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124407 :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dcc952-87b3-5c50-8329-877474c9cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358187} {:answer/id #uuid "7b26dce8-c86e-5f4c-be68-35bd9babb814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358821} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f856b1-f69a-596f-91ca-6e69e6d25660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359482} {:answer/id #uuid "d48510a2-a1cc-5ccc-a78d-0f55b5682108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359606} {:answer/id #uuid "ede18e52-59dd-5738-903a-565892bb8bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ede18e52-59dd-5738-903a-565892bb8bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359866} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f9d5fe-44a3-58bb-a9fc-01470465cbfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360374}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6edb4185-988d-5465-a73c-d87078eb5744" :question/math-academy-id "q-94770" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50fa9289-6d41-5d28-b208-877cbdbfa6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358448} {:answer/id #uuid "667d2986-4910-56e6-9ed3-8f27b9abd76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358649} {:answer/id #uuid "add72146-efe2-5a0e-abaa-a3a2463c3158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359259} {:answer/id #uuid "e28e7362-9db3-53ed-accd-873077451494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+r{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359755} {:answer/id #uuid "ef656522-486e-50e0-b47a-5e62e587789f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "add72146-efe2-5a0e-abaa-a3a2463c3158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359259} :answer-field/id #uuid "aee644e2-ce96-5e60-b711-61139e62f74c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360304}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08bad6ab-1a42-5f7a-8850-36801983c246" :question/math-academy-id "q-95047" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy+{y}^{2}z+x{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12192e9d-666e-5c58-ae9b-549a795d8bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(2z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357907} {:answer/id #uuid "2313e01f-d71c-5200-a515-ee826a71b2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}(z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358058} {:answer/id #uuid "3838a62b-71f1-5444-8110-29c7f4dcb528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358248} {:answer/id #uuid "6d65b027-234d-52ec-b21e-4cda093023c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358706} {:answer/id #uuid "a2650aa0-1dde-559a-991a-ef66b1ee74bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2650aa0-1dde-559a-991a-ef66b1ee74bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359169} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d8dcd8-411e-5bef-bfda-8f715f466b64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360090}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f89a4f6-9145-5fdf-84a8-9fcaed162c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-95171" :question/problem "Given that $u(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $u(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂u}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂u}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z}=2{x}^{2}z+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010406ea-8191-5e47-ac06-dceff2ff745b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357766} {:answer/id #uuid "282feee2-ff23-53af-9799-8744c554abd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358113} {:answer/id #uuid "49f1c912-9ed3-5d04-8e7b-747744cb437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358385} {:answer/id #uuid "6830d01d-5239-5816-9b25-ca0834b037fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358665} {:answer/id #uuid "a16444c4-1302-5cfc-815a-cb0ecda9bea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49f1c912-9ed3-5d04-8e7b-747744cb437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358385} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaff869-51ca-5842-bcc1-9882b7c10798" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360444}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1af069b9-f8b0-59a0-a5fc-f493e760396c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95172" :question/problem "Given that $v(x,y,z)=2y-xz,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $v(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂v}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂v}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂v}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂v}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=-z,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-z, \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta } & =-(-z)r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129511e1-8153-5e51-95ae-9c26ed91e829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357911} {:answer/id #uuid "22e04b8b-94b1-5f23-a25d-237f08e39a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z+{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358054} {:answer/id #uuid "8a775666-2516-5e21-bf4d-75577316d96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358958} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f0ea0f-66cb-5c73-84ba-025ec83912b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359332} {:answer/id #uuid "d256b0d6-11fb-52fc-a283-8f751fba045a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a775666-2516-5e21-bf4d-75577316d96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358958} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e7c9e5-29e9-5ecf-8487-914454d5c63c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7a6bf9a-03da-5b3f-8e6f-6e42220c784c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95174" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=8{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41de3643-e918-53d1-8cb0-5b8e949652ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367923} {:answer/id #uuid "92a45346-b02e-5a67-a6a4-d289bad3bacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367924} {:answer/id #uuid "899dd257-9509-5c24-b797-073d1ba2f7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z+{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367925} {:answer/id #uuid "0e78e572-1929-583c-ac2f-64dd21622fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367926} {:answer/id #uuid "28a698cf-cd11-5929-8167-bd140fb45ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92a45346-b02e-5a67-a6a4-d289bad3bacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367924} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15c034d-fc01-5c57-ac70-f9660ec41f88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "958d3cd5-a585-5cf1-a4ae-62a7c65fe9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-165284" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{2}+xz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2{y}^{2}z+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7772675-d258-5d51-b29d-b3396a5e5001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368894} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7da375-0431-5f66-8543-0491e4f293c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368895} {:answer/id #uuid "43eedeea-a30d-5b9a-ba7b-6945237b5a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368896} {:answer/id #uuid "b844a28f-f811-5210-9c5f-949a6a5243b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z({z}^{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +z{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368897} {:answer/id #uuid "eab23144-3264-5367-8737-fc9bde1fca89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eab23144-3264-5367-8737-fc9bde1fca89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368898} :answer-field/id #uuid "20825cbb-d704-5a1b-b17b-e68c8b914c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368893}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "008135f6-6225-50a5-8c82-16fdb8564c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-95173" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{z}^{4}+{y}^{3}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}{z}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =3{r}^{2}{z}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(3{r}^{2}{z}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373 :question/id #uuid "d178c83a-b8af-5d6a-bf40-fcfb7506feeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6686" :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and $a=60.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all $t \\ge 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127 :topic/math-academy-id 876 :topic/title "Exponential Growth and Decay Models With First-Order ODEs"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f22971a2-d45d-52ec-99fc-a349d577bfb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-0.5(y + 150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121170} {:answer/id #uuid "2435e95e-56ae-5f52-9016-f3f3c23c3ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 150(y + 0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121171} {:answer/id #uuid "36f7d395-bab4-516e-8f87-8da116b30f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 150(y - 0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121172} {:answer/id #uuid "632fa9b9-0103-5768-84d1-c9f8cc114907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0.5(150 - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121173} {:answer/id #uuid "64de0030-8615-5d24-93b0-9b93a8fa45fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0.5(y - 150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121174}] :answer-field/id #uuid "79311994-ead5-5028-b4a4-de97196cfb35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121169}] :question/id #uuid "3ff73a32-c10e-5c6a-a056-14d519235f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-106661" :question/problem "Given that $y < 150$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd57bce4-bf4d-5488-ada6-93439471fb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y^{2} - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121176} {:answer/id #uuid "1f00b80a-9440-52ad-bb3e-9a6954010114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5 - 400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121177} {:answer/id #uuid "34a003e9-7090-500a-bcd5-5a07bde1c2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121178} {:answer/id #uuid "35a91da3-7b8e-54e8-8314-4340d804eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121179} {:answer/id #uuid "6623a9f2-ffe7-561f-9984-2a59cbb33aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121180}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b4164cc7-8b43-5b53-b800-89f7a6270478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4d9520-8eb8-590a-8484-786df4886f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5-400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397851} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2d8367-f11c-56cb-b66f-27a8c632bc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397852} {:answer/id #uuid "906da5cb-d9e8-5ec3-b4fd-cd8abe7cdb40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397853} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1db174-5614-5103-8d7e-cb38f5042fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5{y}^{2}-400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397854} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c36cb8-43dc-59ec-b220-e1d3bce29295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "906da5cb-d9e8-5ec3-b4fd-cd8abe7cdb40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397853} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3476a6-1afc-5395-a095-cd169e19c687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "092a8c01-7fc1-532c-ba20-9cb4c823a717" :question/math-academy-id "q-106662" :question/problem "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a1c9f9-e81b-5cbf-b887-56485f0f137d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(y-250),\\,y(0)=200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241190} {:answer/id #uuid "12bbdcf7-e5f8-5520-a549-ec4ef787a73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241341} {:answer/id #uuid "26fad6ae-6f30-5c85-b6c8-fde919f0e3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241514} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad2cc2d-67d7-52ae-bcdd-b3b3e4ed9748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y(y-250),\\,y(0)=50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241853} {:answer/id #uuid "8a30250b-009d-56da-9a4e-ca03739d84e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-3(y+250),\\,y(0)=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26fad6ae-6f30-5c85-b6c8-fde919f0e3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241514} :answer-field/id #uuid "177efa5e-2726-5c7f-b592-713face07fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f97edbb-38b9-5a3e-9db9-d7a29ce3a4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-106663" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=3,a=250,$ and ${y}_{0}=150.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=400>a=250.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac56bb3-0094-5d6a-8531-71dbd69471d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=5\\,700-0.8P,\\,P(0)=7\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321476} {:answer/id #uuid "a788a3f8-9530-54ec-9aea-383d1c4e9378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.02P-875,\\,P(0)=47\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322459} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a3f83e-f687-58bc-bf83-3f81da84826d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=22P-19\\,734,\\,P(0)=20\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322460} {:answer/id #uuid "ded2b71b-d572-5a02-a041-f687bff48436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322889} {:answer/id #uuid "efa861a6-0396-5d58-8e08-d970115f8b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,250-0.005P,\\,P(0)=270\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded2b71b-d572-5a02-a041-f687bff48436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322889} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bac56d3-4bd4-55bb-aa6f-dc8f9891aec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323386}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09b550d2-2e2e-5317-b1f2-d1a7002c555c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37176" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P=0.003(230\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.003,a=230\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=200\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,250-0.005P=0.005(250\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=270\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5\\,700-0.8P=0.8(7\\,125-P),\\,P(0)=7\\,200\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=270\\,000>a=250\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=7\\,200>a=7\\,125.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07308918-08ed-546a-b64f-46dfcd23d16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=7-0.000\\,2P,\\,P(0)=38\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321204} {:answer/id #uuid "13709d88-4880-5079-9d80-c2aaa3ad82de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=8\\,200-40P,\\,P(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321300} {:answer/id #uuid "188baeac-33cc-5322-843c-5f6944933b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\fra